WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.040
In 2015, the Irish economy grew by&nbsp;
an unprecedented twenty-five percent.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:13.560
In just 12 short months, it achieved&nbsp;
the previous 12 years worth of growth.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.920
Needless to say, that… doesn’t happen. Anywhere.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:28.400
The global average that year was 3.1%. Even China&nbsp;
and India could only manage seven and eight.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.520
And yet 2015 wasn’t the only time Ireland exceeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:40.200
all possible expectations. Six years&nbsp;
later, its economy grew by nearly 14%.

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.200
Today, Ireland is the second-richest country&nbsp;
in the world with a population over one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:54.560
million. Its per-capita GDP of $104,000&nbsp;
is 36% higher than the United States.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.600
Even more impressive is how recent all this is.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.760
Thirty years ago, Ireland lagged slightly behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.320
the European average. Now, it’s&nbsp;
almost three times wealthier.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:12.800
In the 70s, 80s, and 90s, it received&nbsp;
billions of Euros in subsidies from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:18.080
the EU. Today, it contributes billions of Euros.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.800
Back then, thousands fled the country&nbsp;
in search of greener pastures.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:27.000
But since 1996, the flow&nbsp;
of migration has reversed;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.880
skilled professionals from around the globe&nbsp;
now relocate to the island every year.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.720
For all these reasons, Ireland has been&nbsp;
called the “Celtic Tiger” — in reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:45.000
to the rapid rise of the four “Asian Tigers” —&nbsp;
Hong Kong, Taiwan, Singapore, and South Korea.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:51.360
There’s just one tiny problem: it’s not… real.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:59.520
These numbers are all real. And these facts are&nbsp;
technically true. But what they imply is not.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.400
The Irish economic “miracle” is an&nbsp;
elaborate, carefully preserved illusion.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:13.160
To see why, we need to roll back the clock&nbsp;
to 1980, the year everything changed…

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:16.480
Sponsored by GiveWell — the independent nonprofit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:23.360
whose rigorous charity research allows you&nbsp;
and I to make the largest possible impact.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:29.360
In 1980, Apple became the first foreign&nbsp;
tech company to manufacture in Ireland.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.280
According to CEO Tim Cook, it saw in the&nbsp;
country — quote — “a community rich with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.920
talent,” and one that could “accommodate growth.”

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.760
And, in the official corporate version of&nbsp;
history, that’s where the story ends. Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:49.680
took a chance on this small, developing nation&nbsp;
and the two grew hand-in-hand. Apple’s press&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.200
release for its 40th anniversary repeatedly&nbsp;
refers to its Irish “family” and “community”.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:59.840
But the timing tells a different story.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.800
Ireland has pursued business-friendly&nbsp;
policies since at least 1956, when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.840
began offering 10 years of generous&nbsp;
tax breaks to foreign manufacturers.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:14.320
But after joining what would&nbsp;
become the European Union in 1973,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:19.240
it agreed to phase out those&nbsp;
incentives starting in… 1981.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:23.960
Apple, in other words, snuck in the door&nbsp;
right at the eleventh hour — securing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.320
for itself a full decade of tax breaks&nbsp;
during the company’s most vulnerable era.

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:34.360
It was during this period that Steve Jobs&nbsp;
was replaced by the former CEO of Pepsi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:38.840
John Sculley, who initiated a major&nbsp;
restructuring. The company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.640
losing billions of dollars and&nbsp;
bankruptcy was on the horizon.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:50.440
Ireland wasn’t doing much better. Nearly a&nbsp;
quarter of its population was unemployed.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:56.560
So, as its 10-year window began to close,&nbsp;
Apple approached the country with an offer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.200
it would funnel nearly all its worldwide revenue&nbsp;
into Ireland, but it was only willing to pay an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:09.920
effective tax rate of less than 2%. And while 2%&nbsp;
may not be much, that was 2% the country would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:17.680
otherwise never see. In return, Apple would have&nbsp;
a safe place to stash its international profits.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.440
Ireland, desperate for whatever&nbsp;
cash it could get its hands on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.000
accepted this opening bid without negotiation.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:29.160
The only thing left to do was… write the tax code.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.960
To make this arrangement legal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:37.080
it worked backwards, crafting rules that&nbsp;
would artificially produce the 2% figure.

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:41.600
And just like that, Apple&nbsp;
had set its own tax rate.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:47.000
Now, the reason this was&nbsp;
possible was IRS Form-8832.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:53.520
Form-8832 doesn’t look like much — it’s&nbsp;
a pretty unassuming three pages. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.720
completely transformed the American tax landscape.

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.880
Before 8832, U.S. corporations were&nbsp;
required to pay U.S. taxes on their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.840
worldwide income — whether their bananas&nbsp;
were sold in South Carolina or South Africa.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.000
Until recently, that rate was 35%.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.520
After 1996, U.S. corporations&nbsp;
could simply “check a box”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.640
allowing them to avoid paying this tax&nbsp;
as long as their money stayed overseas.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.800
A year later, Ireland passed a similar law. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.080
companies only had to pay tax in the&nbsp;
country where their “key decisions”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:37.720
were made. In practice, anywhere they&nbsp;
wanted. Or, even better, nowhere at all.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.480
The trick was that no one was&nbsp;
enforcing consistency. In America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.800
Apple could argue its subsidiary was&nbsp;
genuinely Irish. Over in Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:53.880
Apple could simultaneously argue it was&nbsp;
really based somewhere else, like Bermuda.

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.880
That was step one.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:06:01.040
Apple had created a low-tax Irish&nbsp;
subsidiary. But it still needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.800
move its international profit into that company.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:08.640
When you walk into an Apple Store&nbsp;
in Munich and buy an iPhone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:14.360
that profit needs to somehow end up&nbsp;
in Apple Ireland, not Apple Germany.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:17.800
The solution was intellectual property.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:23.760
IP is the perfect vehicle for tax avoidance&nbsp;
because it’s (a) extremely valuable — who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.720
could argue that the Apple logo&nbsp;
isn’t worth a fortune? and yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:35.000
(b) extremely difficult to value — whose&nbsp;
to say it’s worth one trillion and not 1.2?

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.360
By moving around these priceless&nbsp;
though intangible patents, logos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:43.600
and other trademarks, Apple&nbsp;
could move its profit, too.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:50.520
It simply gave the rights to use its IP outside&nbsp;
of the Americas to its Irish subsidiary. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.600
whenever it sold an iPhone&nbsp;
in Munich for, say, $100,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:01.480
Apple Germany would have to pay Apple Ireland,&nbsp;
let’s say $98 for use of its trademark. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.560
98% of the profit would be&nbsp;
attributed to low-tax Ireland.

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.040
But… there’s a wrinkle.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.720
Officially, Ireland’s corporate tax rate is 12.5%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:17.800
Recall that to reduce this to less&nbsp;
than two percent, Apple declared its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.840
Irish subsidiary a tax resident of somewhere&nbsp;
like Bermuda, where the rate was even lower.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:31.240
Except… Ireland imposes a special 20% tax on&nbsp;
transfers to “known tax havens”… like Bermuda.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:36.920
Apple, therefore, needed to transfer its money&nbsp;
through an intermediary — another company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:43.040
this time in the Netherlands, to which the&nbsp;
first Irish company would sub-license its IP.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:49.760
Next, Apple created a second Irish company,&nbsp;
which sub-licensed the IP from the Dutch one.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:56.680
Lastly, this second Irish company would license&nbsp;
its IP to Apple subsidiaries across the globe.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.720
Now, all together:

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.640
You walk into a German Apple Store and buy an&nbsp;
iPhone. Apple Germany argues that most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:11.160
value of that sale was derived from intellectual&nbsp;
property — property it doesn’t own. Therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:15.680
Apple Germany pays a “royalty”&nbsp;
to that second Irish company.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:21.920
The second Irish company also doesn’t own&nbsp;
anything. It too pays a fee to the Dutch company.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.160
The Dutch company does the same&nbsp;
— forwarding most of the money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.840
it’s just received onto the first Irish company.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.040
Finally, the first Irish company&nbsp;
claims its “key decisions” are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:39.760
made in a small Caribbean country, where the&nbsp;
corporate tax rate is at or near 0%. Voila!

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:44.080
Apple pioneered this strategy — which&nbsp;
became known as the “Double Irish with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:49.640
Dutch Sandwich” — but it was by no means&nbsp;
the only corporation to take advantage.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:56.240
Google, Microsoft, and Facebook all did&nbsp;
the same. During the 90s and early 2000s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:08:59.200
U.S. multinationals made an&nbsp;
increasing share of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.560
profits overseas — billions of dollars&nbsp;
of which made their way to Ireland.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.280
And while most people assume&nbsp;
some level of tax shenanigans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:13.640
the astonishing scale of this effort&nbsp;
largely flew under the radar for years.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.240
Much of what we know today is only&nbsp;
thanks to independent researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:22.680
and academics like Emma Clancy,&nbsp;
Brian O’Boyle, and Kieran Allen.

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:26.120
Then, in 2013, as the public began taking notice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:31.040
the U.S. Senate held high-profile&nbsp;
hearings, attended by Tim Cook.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:33.720
With the world’s attention focused on Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:39.760
the country feigned innocence. Apple had&nbsp;
simply exploited a “loophole”. Never mind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:44.400
that it had expressly written its&nbsp;
tax code to produce these results.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:49.000
The Double Irish was no more, it&nbsp;
announced. Irish companies would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:53.240
now be taxed in — if you can believe it… Ireland!

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:59.120
Apple’s first Irish subsidiary could no longer&nbsp;
claim to be a “tax resident” of, say, Bermuda.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:06.840
But, not to worry! Generally speaking, tax&nbsp;
treaties supersede local laws. Companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:12.560
therefore, could simply replace Bermuda with a&nbsp;
country that shared a tax treaty with Ireland.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:15.120
For Apple, that ‘country’ was Jersey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:20.520
the British Crown Dependency with a&nbsp;
population of just over 100,000 people.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.200
Problem solved.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:28.560
Now, it should be clear by now that&nbsp;
Ireland is, in fact, a tax haven.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:33.280
The question is: Is its economic success real?

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:37.000
Recall that Ireland is one of the&nbsp;
richest countries on the planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:46.200
with a per-capita GDP of over $100,000, and&nbsp;
that its economy grew by 25% in 2015 alone.

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:52.200
Critics of its tax regime argue that this is&nbsp;
all just a statistical mirage — that the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.080
is little more than a Bermuda, Bahamas,&nbsp;
or British Virgin Islands with castles.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:05.040
Consider, for example, what happened in&nbsp;
2015. Irish workers weren’t suddenly 25%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:11.440
more productive. Nor did Irish&nbsp;
companies export 25% more goods.

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:14.920
No, what really happened is that&nbsp;
when Ireland closed the “Double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:19.760
Irish” “loophole”, it opened&nbsp;
a new one. Starting in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:25.440
companies could subtract from their tax&nbsp;
bill the full cost of any IP they purchased.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:33.000
So, what did companies do? They “bought” a&nbsp;
whole lot of IP. (From themselves, of course)

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.200
Companies like Apple transferred an estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:41.320
$270 billion worth of trademarks and&nbsp;
patents into their Irish subsidiaries.

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.880
And since on paper, trademarks are no&nbsp;
different from oil or refrigerators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:50.080
Ireland’s GDP grew by 25%.

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:54.760
This is what skeptics mean when&nbsp;
they say its economy isn’t “real”.

00:11:54.760 --> 00:12:00.480
Even worse, while the benefits aren’t&nbsp;
real, they argue, there are real costs.

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:03.160
The dominance of foreign multinationals in tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:09.200
Ireland has contributed to a housing crisis&nbsp;
that makes America’s look tame by comparison.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:19.400
Between 1991 and 2007, Dublin home prices&nbsp;
increased by 429%, according to these researchers.

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.640
Secondly, the Irish economy&nbsp;
is incredibly dependent on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:26.360
a very small number of American corporations.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:30.360
Just ten are responsible for 60%&nbsp;
of the country’s corporate tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:35.240
receipts. The top three pay&nbsp;
about a third of the total.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:39.560
From the recent boom to the busts of the&nbsp;
Great Recession and the DotCom bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:43.840
the Irish economy is fully&nbsp;
at the mercy of Washington.

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:50.320
In 2021, Ireland was pressured into signing an&nbsp;
OECD agreement that sets a 15% global minimum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:56.000
corporate tax rate and re-allocates profits to&nbsp;
the country in which a sale physically occurred.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.920
If the past is any indication, Ireland will&nbsp;
simply invent a new “loophole”. Still, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:08.200
risk remains that it will one day truly be forced&nbsp;
to change its ways and lose out on this revenue.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:10.720
And not just revenue.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:14.640
As proponents of Ireland’s tax&nbsp;
regime are quick to point out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:18.720
part of the implicit bargain struck&nbsp;
nearly 40 years ago between Apple and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:24.080
Ireland was that the former provide&nbsp;
high-quality highly-paid employment.

00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:28.400
The United States, along with much of the&nbsp;
world, experienced a baby boom following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:33.480
the end of World War II. Twenty years&nbsp;
later, these millions of young adults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:39.480
joined the workforce — producing this&nbsp;
steady, 40-year rise in employment.

00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:46.040
Ireland’s baby boom, on the other hand, was about&nbsp;
30-years delayed and much more compressed. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:52.040
you can see, it suddenly had millions of&nbsp;
young, working-age people in the 1990s.

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:57.320
Apple and its peers put thousands&nbsp;
of these 20 and 30 years to work.

00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:00.640
Today, as much as 10% of the Irish workforce is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:06.840
employed by foreign companies — nearly&nbsp;
all American and nearly all in tech.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:11.040
Apple’s 2020 press release about&nbsp;
its Irish “community” wasn’t a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:16.520
total smokescreen — it truly does&nbsp;
employ more than just accountants.

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:22.040
Even if low taxes brought multinationals&nbsp;
in the first place, this argument goes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.040 --> 00:14:26.240
they’ve stayed for its genuine&nbsp;
operational advantages.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:29.600
Indeed, Ireland is now the only&nbsp;
English-speaking country in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:36.240
EU. It’s a mere six hours from New York&nbsp;
and its population is highly-educated.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:43.200
Sure, Ireland is a tax haven, they might&nbsp;
admit, but its economic success is very real.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:48.040
But what both those who argue Ireland’s&nbsp;
economy is ‘real’ and those who argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:54.120
it’s ‘fake’ misunderstand is that “real”&nbsp;
and “fake” aren’t contradictory. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.120 --> 00:14:57.920
these two sides of its economy are complementary.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:01.640
Take, for instance, what happened in 2013.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:04.640
Shortly after the explosive U.S. Senate hearings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:09.440
the European Union launched an&nbsp;
investigation into Apple’s tax practices.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:15.360
And in 2016, it concluded that Ireland had&nbsp;
granted it special treatment. And while it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:19.520
of course, entitled to set its own&nbsp;
tax laws, as a member of the EU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:24.680
it also agrees not to grant preferential&nbsp;
treatment to any one corporation.

00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:29.120
The court — in a ruling that has since been&nbsp;
overturned and is now in the process of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:35.920
second appeal — ordered Apple to pay&nbsp;
Ireland $14 billion in unpaid taxes.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:40.560
So, how did Ireland react to&nbsp;
this $14 billion windfall?

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:42.320
With outrage.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.680
To the confusion of many Irish citizens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:48.520
their government launched an&nbsp;
immediate challenge to this ruling.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:52.800
Now, you might be thinking “Of course&nbsp;
— better to turn down a small sum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:57.000
money today to preserve the larger flow tomorrow.”

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:00.760
But this was no “small” amount of money. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:06.160
Ireland is a country of just 5 million&nbsp;
people. $14 Billion would’ve paid for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:11.640
country’s entire annual health budget&nbsp;
or seven years of transportation.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:13.840
Let’s not forget that lower corporate tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:18.240
rates are merely an alternative&nbsp;
means of achieving the same goal.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:22.680
The Irish government — like every other&nbsp;
government — is in the business of collecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:30.120
more taxes. So why turn down so much cash?&nbsp;
Especially since, in the eyes of Apple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:36.080
Ireland’s hands were clean — it didn’t&nbsp;
initiate the court case, the EU did.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:40.440
Because… Ireland is not just a tax haven.

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:44.360
After all, there are safer, cheaper,&nbsp;
and more secretive places to stash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.360 --> 00:16:50.520
your profits. It isn’t even the lowest-tax&nbsp;
jurisdiction in Europe! Apple could surely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:56.520
find a more “accommodating” government in a&nbsp;
smaller, less democratic Caribbean island.

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:03.480
What Ireland really sells — and what the Caribbean&nbsp;
can’t offer — is its untarnished reputation.

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:07.480
Yes, the ultra-low taxes&nbsp;
of the Cayman Islands. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:11.800
without the connotation of “the Cayman Islands”.

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.080
Here’s how it works:

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:19.800
First, Ireland’s real economy — the one in which&nbsp;
real people go to work every day and fix pipes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:25.480
connect servers, and sell computers&nbsp;
— creates the illusion of legitimacy.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:27.280
Second, thanks to this illusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:33.440
companies concerned about their image — like&nbsp;
Apple — choose Ireland as their tax shelter.

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.560
Third, in doing so, Apple inflates the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:41.760
economic health — creating the&nbsp;
appearance of unbelievable wealth.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:47.120
Fourth and finally, this “economic miracle”&nbsp;
is seized upon by those with an agenda to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.120 --> 00:17:53.720
lower corporate tax rates, who argue “look what’s&nbsp;
possible when you’re just friendly to businesses”.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.640
Without the “real”, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:01.440
the “fake” wouldn’t exist. And without&nbsp;
the “fake”, neither would the “real”.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:07.360
In that 2013 Senate hearing, Tim Cook proudly&nbsp;
proclaimed, quote, “Apple does not hold money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:13.320
on a Caribbean Island,” and, quote, “does not&nbsp;
have a bank account in the Cayman Islands”.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:18.160
That was true. And that’s&nbsp;
why Apple chooses Ireland.

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.400
The reason Ireland fought the&nbsp;
EU ruling was that something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:25.520
more important than $14 billion was at stake.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:31.040
Something extraordinarily valuable, yet&nbsp;
impossible to precisely value — much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:37.480
like the intellectual property Apple moves&nbsp;
between its subsidiaries — its reputation.

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:41.160
In truth, many of the things we&nbsp;
value the most are also the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.160 --> 00:18:46.080
difficult to measure — happiness,&nbsp;
love, creativity, and doing “good”.

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:50.760
We all want to do the most “good”.&nbsp;
But when we give, say, to a charity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:54.840
how do we ensure that we’re making&nbsp;
the most effective possible impact?

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:59.400
Well, in 2007, a few friends had&nbsp;
that same question and decided to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:04.680
start GiveWell. They closely scrutinize&nbsp;
all the data we have on charities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.720
publish this information on the GiveWell website,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:15.080
allowing you and I to compare the over 1.5 million&nbsp;
nonprofit organizations in the United States.

00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:20.720
That way, when we go to donate, we know where&nbsp;
our money is going, what it’s doing, and who it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:27.440
helping. These are non-partisan, cost-effective,&nbsp;
evidence-backed causes like preventing malaria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:32.320
solving vitamin deficiencies, and&nbsp;
incentivizing childhood vaccines.

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:37.000
We shouldn’t have to become experts on&nbsp;
charitable giving. When we shop for phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:43.760
or cars, we search for reviews. So why not&nbsp;
outsource that work to charity researchers?

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:48.640
What I like about GiveWell is that they don’t&nbsp;
take any fees. All of your money goes straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:53.880
to the charity of your choice. They’ve even&nbsp;
gone out of their way to advise me not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.880 --> 00:20:00.080
disparage other charities, which to me is&nbsp;
a pretty clear indicator of their values.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:04.440
Plus, right now, new donors can get&nbsp;
their entire donation matched — up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:10.760
$100 — before the end of the year, as long&nbsp;
as funds last. So hurry now while you still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:17.960
can and go to Givewell.org or click the&nbsp;
link in the description or on screen now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:26.640
then choose “YouTube” and enter “PolyMatter”&nbsp;
at checkout to do twice as much good.

